lesson: 198
title: Exam 1: two exchanges
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: «If A gives B 6, they are equal» means A has 2 × 6 = 12 more than B.
explain: «If instead B gives A 6» makes the gap 12 + 6 + 6 = 24. If A then has 3 times as many as B, the gap is 2 of B's units: one unit is 24 ÷ 2 = 12.
explain: So after giving, B has 12; before, B had 12 + 6 = 18, and A had 18 + 12 = 30. Check: 24 and 24; 36 and 12.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing g=2..12 m=2..5
let: y = g * (m + 3) / (m - 1)
need: y - g >= 2
let: x = y + 2 * g
answer: x
exercise: If {a} gives {b} {g} {t}, they will have the same number. If instead {b} gives {a} {g} {t}, {a} will have {m} times as many as {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: Two shelves hold books. If {g} books move from the top shelf to the bottom one, the shelves hold equal numbers. If {g} books move from the bottom shelf to the top one, the top shelf holds {m} times as many as the bottom. How many books are on the top shelf?
exercise: There are birds on two trees. If {g} birds fly from the first tree to the second, both trees have the same number of birds. If {g} birds fly from the second tree to the first, the first tree has {m} times as many birds as the second. How many birds are on the first tree?
exercise: {a} and {b} have some dollars. If {a} gives {b} {g} dollars, they will have the same amount. If {b} gives {a} {g} dollars, {a} will have {m} times as much as {b}. How many dollars does {a} have?
exercise: Two boxes hold {t}. Moving {g} {t} from the first box to the second makes the boxes equal, but moving {g} {t} from the second box to the first makes the first box hold {m} times as many as the second. How many {t} are in the first box?
test: Were {b} to pass {g} {t} to {a}, {a}'s {t} would be {m} times {b}'s. Were {a} to pass {g} {t} to {b}, they would be even. What number of {t} does {a} hold?
test: Two pens hold sheep. Taking {g} sheep from the left pen into the right pen evens them out. Taking {g} sheep the other way leaves the left pen with {m} times as many sheep as the right pen. How many sheep are in the left pen?
test: {a} says to {b}: «Give me {g} of your {t}, and I will have {m} times as many as you.» {b} answers: «Better give me {g} of yours, and we will have the same number.» How many {t} does {a} have?
test: How many children are in the first group if moving {g} children from the first group to the second makes the groups equal, while moving {g} children from the second group to the first makes the first group {m} times as large?
exercise: If {b} gives {a} {g} {t}, {a} will have {m} times as many {t} as {b}. If {a} gives {b} {g} {t}, they will have equal numbers. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: How many {t} does {a} have, if giving {b} {g} {t} would make them equal, and getting {g} {t} from {b} would give {a} {m} times as many as {b}?
exercise: Two classrooms in a school in Texas have chairs. Moving {g} chairs from the first room to the second makes them equal. Moving {g} chairs from the second room to the first gives the first room {m} times as many as the second. How many chairs are in the first room?
exercise: {a} gives {b} {g} {t}: equal. {b} gives {a} {g} {t}: {a} has {m} times as many. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: Two buses in Ontario carry passengers. If {g} passengers move from bus A to bus B, the buses carry the same number. If {g} passengers move from bus B to bus A, bus A carries {m} times as many as bus B. How many passengers are on bus A?
exercise: {a} and {b} in Liverpool collect {t}. If {a} hands {b} {g}, they have the same amount. If {b} hands {a} {g}, {a}'s pile is {m} times {b}'s. How many {t} are in {a}'s pile?
exercise: Two fish tanks in a pet shop in Sydney hold fish. Moving {g.words} fish from the first tank to the second evens them out; moving {g.words} fish from the second tank to the first makes the first hold {m} times as many. How many fish are in the first tank?
exercise: There are two piles of {t}. Taking {g} from the first pile to the second makes them equal; taking {g} from the second to the first makes the first pile {m} times the second. How many {t} are in the first pile?
test: {a} has more {t} than {b}. Should {a} pass {g} {t} to {b}, they would be level. Should {b} pass {g} {t} to {a}, {a} would have {m} times as many as {b}. How many {t} has {a} got?
test: In a school in Dublin there are two choirs. If {g} singers go from choir one to choir two, the choirs are equal; if {g} singers go from choir two to choir one, choir one is {m} times as large. How many singers are in choir one?
test: Find how many {t} {a} owns if after {a} gives {g} to {b} both own the same, but after {b} gives {g} to {a}, {a} owns {m} times as many as {b}.
test: Two wagons on a farm in Iowa carry bales of hay. Shifting {g} bales from the first wagon onto the second evens the loads, while shifting {g} bales the opposite way makes the first wagon carry {m} times as many bales. How many bales are on the first wagon?
test: {b} tells {a}: «If you give me {g} {t}, we will be equal.» {a} replies: «If you give me {g}, I will have {m} times as many as you.» How many {t} does {a} have?
test: Two baskets at a market in New Zealand hold eggs. When {g} eggs go from the first basket to the second, both baskets hold the same; when {g} eggs go from the second to the first, the first holds {m} times as many. How many eggs are in the first basket?
