exam: It is given that, were {a} to transfer {g} {t} to {b}, the holdings of the two would be equal; whereas, were {b} instead to transfer {g} {t} to {a}, the holding of {a} would be {m} times that of {b}. Determine the number of {t} held by {a}.
exam: Two shelves hold books. Should {g} books be removed from the upper shelf to the lower, the shelves would hold equal numbers; should {g} books instead be removed from the lower shelf to the upper, the upper would hold {m} times as many as the lower. Ascertain the number of books upon the upper shelf.
exam: Whereas the transfer of {g} from a first quantity to a second renders the two equal, and the transfer of {g} from the second to the first renders the first {m} times the second, state the value of the first quantity.
exam: Compute the number of {t} in the possession of {a}, given the following two conditions: a gift of {g} {t} from {a} to {b} would equalise their holdings, and a gift of {g} {t} from {b} to {a} would cause the holding of {a} to be {m} times that of {b}.
exercise: Grandad passed away and left his books to {a} and {b}. If {a} gives {b} {g} books, they have the same number. If {b} gives {a} {g} books, {a} has {m} times as many as {b}. How many books did {a} get?
exercise: {b} was creative with the truth and said they had the same number of {t}. Not quite: if {a} gives {b} {g} {t}, then they will. And if {b} gives {a} {g} {t}, {a} will have {m} times as many. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: {a}'s 'borrowed' some of {b}'s {t} (nicked em). if {a} hands back {g} they're even; if {b} gives {a} {g} more, {a}'s got {m} times as many. how many {t} has {a} got, smart arse?
